  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">European Union-Cambodia partnership for national agricultural laboratory</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Cambodia#s agricultural sector is a cornerstone of its economy, yet its sustainable growth and export potential are significantly constrained by systemic challenges, including those in food safety compliance.  A critical bottleneck lies in the limited capacity of testing laboratories in Cambodia and their lack of ISO/IEC 17025 accreditation. This gap restricts reliable and timely testing, undermines public health, and limits Cambodia#s trade readiness and market access.  Currently, MAFF operates four laboratories with outdated equipment and limited technical capacity, conducting less than 25% of the analyses required to fulfil its mandates. These facilities lack coordination and accreditation, resulting in high operational costs, low productivity, and limited-service delivery. Only two laboratories nationwide hold ISO/IEC 17025 accreditation, both with narrow scopes, prompting many FBOs and institutions to seek testing services abroad, despite a strong preference for reliable domestic options.  To address these gaps, the Royal Government of Cambodia is establishing the National Agriculture Laboratory in Setbo (NALS), a centralized, modern facility aligned with the Cambodia Pentagonal Strategy 2024#2028 and the National Agriculture Development Policy 2022#2030. NALS will consolidate existing MAFF laboratories, e</narrative>
  </description>
